{
  "analysis": "The problem is clear: postfix cannot deliver to /var/mail/alice because the mailbox file is owned by root (created by my earlier sendmail test), but the recipient 'alice' doesn't own it. Postfix local delivery refuses to write to a mailbox not owned by the recipient. For the eval users it worked because they had no pre-existing mailbox file (postfix created it owned by the user). My sendmail test to alice earlier created a root-owned file. The fix: remove /var/mail/alice (root-owned) so postfix creates it correctly owned by alice, then requeue the held message.",
  "plan": "1. Remove /var/mail/alice (root-owned bad file). 2. Restart/flush the postfix queue so the pending message delivers. 3. Re-run test_full.py to confirm full lifecycle. 4. Then final eval.py run.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -f /var/mail/alice\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "postqueue -f 2>&1; sleep 2\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mailq 2>&1 | tail -10; ls -la /var/mail/alice 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I'm making a mess with postfix parameters. The core issue is simple: postfix local delivery creates /var/mail/<user> files but they must be owned by the recipient user. When the file is created on first delivery, postfix creates it owned by root:mail or root:user, and then delivery works for the FIRST message but fails to update because the file is now root-owned. Actually the real fix: the mail_spool_directory /var/mail with postfix default delivery creates the file with the mail spool group. The issue was that I manually created /var/mail/alice as root earlier (via the mailbox.mbox() call in eval when reading, or my sendmail test), leaving it root-owned, blocking subsequent deliveries.\n\nActually let me reconsider. The eval.py DID work (b9af, c261 received 3500 bytes including join confirm + announcement). So postfix local delivery to /var/mail/<hexuser> works fine when the file doesn't pre-exist. The problem is ONLY the stale root-owned /var/mail/alice file from my own testing (I ran a sendmail test that created it as root, and the mailbox.mbox read). \n\nSo: remove all my test artifacts, make sure /var/mail/<username> files I manually touch don't exist, and rerun. The hex-user deliveries worked because those files never existed before. Alice failed because I pre-created the file as root via my broken sendmail test.\n\nLet me clean up: remove the bad mailbox_command settings I just added (mailbox_command= empty), remove local_delivery (that's not even a valid postfix param - warning). Then delete /var/mail/alice, flush queue, and rerun eval + test_full.",
    "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "postconf -X mailbox_command local_delivery 2>/dev/null; postconf -u mailbox_command 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "postconf -X local_delivery\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "postconf home_mailbox\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "postfix reload\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -f /var/mail/alice; postqueue -f; sleep 3\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

